# PHASE 1: P0 BLOCKERS (core features that do not work today)

These were found by tracing UI → service → rules. Together they mean regular members almost certainly **cannot check in at all**, admins **cannot create sessions**, and applicants **cannot accept invitations**. Fix them in this order.

## P0-1. Session data model drift (PARTLY FIXED in v2, still incomplete) `[CODE]`
**v2 status:** the form now sends `startAt`/`endAt`/`timezone`, so creation no longer throws. **Still missing:** `checkInMode`, `openMinutesBefore`, `graceMinutesAfter`, `lateAfterMinutes` are written nowhere, yet `sessionOpen()` in the rules reads them, so every member self check-in is still denied even after the other fixes. Also keep the remaining items below (validation, `points` 0, defaults, migration). The description that follows is the original finding; treat the first bullet as resolved.
- `NewSessionModal.handleSubmit` builds a payload with only `date`, `startTime`, `endTime` (strings) and no `startAt`, `endAt`, `timezone`, `checkInMode`, `openMinutesBefore`, `graceMinutesAfter`, `lateAfterMinutes`.
- `createSessionInFirestore` immediately calls `(sessionData.startAt as Timestamp).toMillis` → `TypeError` on `undefined`, so creating a session **throws**. The seed session in `handleSeedClubTemplate` has the same problem (the error is swallowed as a console warning).
- `firebase-blueprint.json` (required: startAt, endAt, timezone, checkInMode) and `firestore.rules` (`isValidSession` requires `timezone`; `sessionOpen()` reads `startAt`, `endAt`, `openMinutesBefore`, `graceMinutesAfter`) expect the richer model. The UI and the rules are out of sync.
- On edit, changing `date`/`startTime`/`endTime` would not update `startAt`/`endAt`, so displayed time and enforced window diverge.

**Required fix:**
1. In `NewSessionModal`, keep the date/time inputs but derive the stored fields on save using the existing `parseDateTimeInZone(date, time, tz)`:
   `startAt`, `endAt` as Firestore `Timestamp`; `timezone` from club settings or `DEFAULT_CLUB_TIMEZONE` ('Asia/Seoul'); `checkInMode: 'auto'`; `openMinutesBefore`, `graceMinutesAfter`, `lateAfterMinutes` from `DEFAULT_CHECKIN_SETTINGS`. Keep `startTime`/`endTime`/`date` as display fields derived from the same inputs.
2. Validate in the form AND in `createSessionInFirestore`/`updateSessionInFirestore`: title/location/host length limits, `endAt > startAt`, date is a valid `YYYY-MM-DD`, points is an integer in 0..1000 and **0 is allowed** (today `Number(points) || 20` silently turns 0 into 20). Use one shared default for points (`DEFAULT_CHECKIN_SETTINGS.defaultPoints`); the code currently uses 10, 20 and 25 in different places.
3. Stop defaulting a brand-new session's `status` to `'active'`. Use `'scheduled'`. Keep `'active'`, `'upcoming'`, `'completed'`, `'cancelled'` valid for old data.
4. `updateSessionInFirestore` must send `isRescheduled = true` when date, start or end time changed, so the reschedule announcement is created (nothing passes it today).
5. Extend `isValidSession()` in the rules: `startAt is timestamp`, `endAt is timestamp`, `endAt > startAt`, `timezone is string`, `checkInMode in ['auto','open','closed']`, numeric ranges for the three minute fields (0..1440), `points` int 0..1000, size limits on strings, and a `hasOnly` field whitelist covering every field in the `Session` type.
6. Add the migration described in DATA MIGRATION RULES so existing sessions get the missing fields.

## P0-2. Self check-in can never succeed for regular members `[CODE]`
Three independent defects stack up:
- **(a) Token is discarded.** `QRScannerModal` parses `decoded.token` but never passes it on. `handleRecordCheckIn` → `recordCheckInAtomic` → `recordSelfCheckIn(..., token = 'DIRECT_VERIFIED', ...)`. The rules' `tokenValid()` can never match that string, so the write is rejected.
- **(b) Deep link is captured but never consumed.** `captureAndStoreDeepLink()` stores `{sessionId, token}` in `sessionStorage` and scrubs the URL. `getPendingCheckIn()` and `clearPendingCheckIn()` are **never called anywhere**. A phone's native camera opening `/?s=...&t=...`, then signing in, does nothing. Also `App`'s "open scanner on `?verify/sessionId/checkin`" effect runs after the URL has been scrubbed and the param names do not match (`s`/`t`).
- **(c) Pre-read on a non-existent doc.** `recordSelfCheckIn`/`recordCheckInAtomic` call `getDoc(checkIns/{sessionId}_{memberId})` first. The `checkIns` read rule dereferences `resource.data.memberId`; for a document that does not exist `resource` is `null`, so the read is denied and the function throws `permission-denied` before the write is even attempted.

**Required fix:**
1. Carry the token end to end: `onRecordCheckIn(sessionId, memberId, method, notes?, token?)`. The scanner passes `decoded.token`. Manual code entry (the 8-char backup code, `kind: 'backup_code'`) must resolve to a session and pass the token. In the backup-code case the member must pick or confirm the session; currently the backup code is matched against `sessionCode`/`id`, which is a different thing.
2. Consume the deep link: after login and when the member is `active`, read `getPendingCheckIn()`, call the check-in with it, then `clearPendingCheckIn()` whether it succeeds or fails. Show a clear result in `AttendanceConfirmationModal`. If the user is not yet approved, keep the pending link for up to 10 minutes and show a "sign in / awaiting approval" message instead of failing silently.
3. Remove the pre-read. Do **create-first**: `setDoc(checkInRef, record)`. If it fails with `permission-denied`, then `getDoc(checkInRef)` (now allowed for the owner because the doc exists); if it exists → return `isDuplicate: true`; otherwise map the failure to a human message (check-in closed, token expired, not an active member). Do the same in `recordAdminManualCheckIn`.
4. Make `recordCheckInAtomic` honest: it is not atomic today. Either use `runTransaction` or (preferred, since the deterministic ID + `allow update: if false` already gives atomic create-once semantics) rename it and document that.
5. Admins who are also members: when an admin checks in **themselves** through the scanner, use the self path (token required), not the manual path. Right now `operatorUid = isAdmin ? uid : undefined` sends admins through `manual_entry`, which bypasses token and window checks.

## P0-3. Check-in rules: token bypass, clock-skew rejection, mutable fields `[CODE]`
Update the `checkIns` create rule and helpers to this shape (adapt names, keep behavior):

```
function tokenValid(sid, t) {
  let k = get(/databases/$(database)/documents/sessionTokens/$(sid)).data;
  return t is string && t.size() == 8 && (
    (t == k.token && request.time <= k.rotatedAt + duration.value(75, 's')) ||
    (k.prevToken is string && k.prevToken.size() == 8 && t == k.prevToken &&
     request.time <= k.rotatedAt + duration.value(30, 's'))
  );
}
```
- **Why:** the kiosk's first write stores `prevToken: ''`. Today a member submitting an empty-string token matches `t == k.prevToken` for 30 s after the kiosk starts, so anyone can check in without scanning. `[CODE]`
- **Status computation:** the rule requires `status == (request.time > startAt + lateAfter ? 'late' : 'present')`, but the client computes `status` from the device clock. Any clock skew or a check-in within seconds of the boundary gets `permission-denied`. Replace the equality with a ±15 s tolerance band: accept `'present'` if `request.time <= lateAt + 15s`, accept `'late'` if `request.time >= lateAt - 15s`. On the client, derive the status from a server-synchronised time (add `GET /api/time` to `server.ts` returning `Date.now()`, compute the offset once at startup of the scanner, and use it in `getSessionState` for check-in decisions).
- **Field whitelist (member branch):** `request.resource.data.keys().hasOnly(['id','sessionId','memberId','authUid','method','token','checkedInAt','status','monthKey'])`; `method in ['camera_scan','code_input','kiosk_scan']`; `monthKey == date.split('-')[0] + '-' + date.split('-')[1]` of the session's `date`. Today a member can write arbitrary `monthKey`, `track`, `verifiedBy`, `notes`, `method`, which feeds directly into monthly statistics (`calculateMonthlyStats` trusts `c.monthKey` first). `[CODE]`
- **Admin branch:** require `exists(members/{memberId})`, `exists(sessions/{sessionId})`, `checkedInAt == request.time`, `verifiedBy == request.auth.uid`, `status in ['present','late']`, and a `hasOnly` whitelist that adds `notes`, `verifiedBy`. `recordAdminManualCheckIn` currently writes `checkedInAt: new Date().toISOString()` (a string) while self check-ins use `serverTimestamp()`. Use `serverTimestamp()` everywhere; keep the reader code (`toJsDate`) tolerant of both for old data.
- **Server-side close must actually close:** the kiosk's "close check-in" calls `handleUpdateSessionStatus(id, 'completed')`, which only changes `status`. The rules only block on `status == 'cancelled'` or `checkInMode == 'closed'`, so members can keep checking in until the grace window ends. Make it call `completeSession()` (sets `status: 'completed'` AND `checkInMode: 'closed'`), and make `sessionOpen()` also reject `status == 'completed'`.
- **Client/server window parity:** `getSessionState` treats legacy `status === 'active'` as open regardless of time, and `'completed'` as closed; the rules ignore both and use only the time window. Make the client mirror the rules exactly: cancelled → closed; `checkInMode == 'closed'` or completed → closed; `'open'` → open; otherwise time window. Remove the unconditional `'active'` shortcut (or migrate old `'active'` sessions to `checkInMode: 'auto'` in the migration). Also fix `QRScannerModal`'s `status !== 'active'` gate, which blocks valid `'scheduled'` sessions that are inside their window.

## P0-4. Member invitation acceptance fails for every applicant `[CODE]`
`acceptMembershipInvitation` does `getDoc(members/{approvedMemberId})` before the write. The `members` read rule only allows admins and **active** members; the applicant is still `approved_waiting_response`, so the read throws `permission-denied` (outside any try/catch) and the user sees a raw error. The same pre-read pattern exists in `approveUserMembershipLink` (fine for admins).
**Required fix:** remove the applicant-side member read. The accept flow becomes a single `writeBatch`: update `users/{uid}` (status → `active`, memberId, respondedAt, updatedAt) and update `members/{approvedMemberId}` (`authUid` only), with no reads. The rules already validate both against the staged `approvedMemberId` (rules read pre-batch state, which is what is needed). Move the "member exists and is not linked to another account" validation to the **approve** step (admin side), and have `approveUserMembershipLink` refuse a non-existent member (today it will happily stage an ID that does not exist). Add a rules test for the complete approve → accept → check-in chain.

## P0-5. Session deletion is impossible `[CODE]`
`match /sessions/{id}` uses `allow write: if isAdmin() && ... isValidSession(request.resource.data)`. On delete, `request.resource` is `null`, so the rule errors and denies. `deleteSession()` can never succeed. In addition, `RecordDeleteModal` promises "this will also permanently purge all associated check-in records", but `deleteSession` deletes only the session doc (orphaning check-ins, announcements and the token doc), and `adminDeleteSession` ignores its `permanent` flag.
**Required fix:** split the rule: `allow create, update: if isAdmin() && isValidId(sessionId) && isValidSession(request.resource.data);` and `allow delete: if isAdmin();`. Default behavior = **soft delete** (set `status: 'cancelled'` + `deletedAt`, hide from default lists) so history is preserved. A "permanent" delete first deletes related `checkIns` (query by `sessionId`, in batches of ≤ 400), the `sessionTokens/{id}` doc and related announcements, then the session, with progress and error reporting. Make the modal text match what really happens, with the real count. Write an audit entry.

## P0-6. Members and sessions are subscribed to the wrong way for non-admins `[CODE]`
- `App` subscribes to the **entire** `checkIns` collection for every signed-in user. The rule only allows a member to read their own check-ins, and Firestore rules do not filter, so a non-admin collection listener is **denied**. Result: members see a red "Could not sync attendance records" banner, and their home stats/history are empty. `subscribeToMemberCheckIns(memberId)` exists but is **unused**.
- All three listeners (members, sessions, check-ins) also start for `pending`/`declined`/`suspended` users, who have no read access, producing permission-denied noise and an error banner.
**Required fix:** start the data listeners only when `membershipState === 'active'` or `isAdmin`. Admins → full listeners (see Phase 3 for bounding). Members → `subscribeToMemberCheckIns(linkedMember.id)` plus members/sessions. Pass the right data to the views, and make views that need all check-ins (Participation, leaderboard computation) admin-only or backed by the public leaderboard projection. Add `onError` handlers (the `AuthContext` member listener has none).

## P0-7. Public leaderboard is never populated; opt-out does nothing `[CODE]`
- `recomputeAndSaveLeaderboard()` is **never called**. The `publicLeaderboard` collection stays empty, and guests see a leaderboard built from `members` state that is empty for signed-out visitors. The fallback formula (`totalCheckIns*10 + currentStreak*5`, using member fields nothing ever updates) disagrees with `computeMemberStats` (sum of `session.points`).
- `leaderboardOptOut` exists in rules, types and the projection filter, but **no UI sets it**, and recompute only `set`s docs for included members and never deletes the public doc of someone who opted out or went Inactive, so previously published data stays public.
**Required fix:** (1) Add an opt-out toggle to `EditProfileModal` and include `leaderboardOptOut` in `updateMemberSelfProfile`. (2) Add a rule: `allow delete: if isAdmin() || (isActiveMember() && memberId == myMemberId());` on `publicLeaderboard/{memberId}` and have the client delete its own projection when opting out. (3) Make the admin client run a debounced recompute (e.g. when admin data changes, at most once per 5 min, plus a manual "Refresh leaderboard" button) that also deletes projection docs for opted-out/inactive/deleted members. (4) Remove the invented fallback formula; with an empty collection show an honest empty state. (5) Optional, requires billing: note a scheduled Cloud Function as the long-term source of truth, but do not implement it.

## P0-8. Reminders: wrong actor, quota burn, and resurrecting notifications `[CODE]`
`checkAndSend30MinuteReminders` runs in **every** signed-in client every 60 s. It reads **all** sessions each time (`S × clients × 1,440` reads/day; e.g. 50 sessions × 30 clients is ~90,000 reads/hour, enough to exhaust the Spark free tier of 50,000 reads/day in minutes). For non-admins it then fails on `getDocs(users)` (admin-only) and swallows the error. For admins it does sequential `getDoc`+`setDoc` per (session × user). `dismissNotification` deletes the doc, and the next sweep recreates it (deterministic ID, `!exists`), so dismissed reminders come back every minute.
**Required fix:** (1) Run the sweep **only for admins**, and only while the tab is visible. (2) Query only upcoming sessions (`where('date', '>=', today)` in the club timezone) instead of the whole collection. (3) Use one `writeBatch` per sweep. (4) Replace delete-on-dismiss with a `dismissed: true` flag (owner update allowed), and have the sweep skip sessions where the notification doc already exists in any state. (5) Show members a notice that reminders depend on an admin device being online, and document that a scheduled Cloud Function is the reliable alternative (optional, billing).

