# PHASE 2: P1 SECURITY AND DATA INTEGRITY

## Security
- **S-1 Identity spoofing at signup `[CODE]`:** `users` create validates only the shape; `email`/`displayName` are free text, not tied to the token. A pending user can claim any email, which the admin approval screen then shows as verified. Require `request.resource.data.email.lower() == request.auth.token.email.lower()` and `request.auth.token.email_verified == true` on create, and show the **auth** email in the approval UI.
- **S-2 Missing schema/size validation `[CODE]`:** add `hasOnly` + size/type checks for `notifications` create (owner: `userId == uid`, allowed `type` values, field sizes; owner update only `read`/`dismissed`), `memberPrivate` (email, phone, university, department, major, semester; each ≤ 150 chars; owner can only touch their own), `admins` docs (email, grantedBy, grantedAt), `announcements`, `settings/club`, `members` create (use `isValidMember`), and `bio ≤ 500`, `avatarUrl` must be empty or an `https://` URL ≤ 500 chars (an arbitrary image URL on the public leaderboard lets any member embed a third-party tracking pixel), each `tracks` item in the allowed list. Normalise the role list so rules, `BUILDER_ROLES` and `isValidMember` agree (rules currently accept extra roles `Founder, Lead, Builder, Apprentice` that the UI cannot set).
- **S-3 Audit log integrity `[CODE]`:** audit entries are written after the operation, best effort (`catch → console.warn`), with a **client** ISO timestamp, so they can be missing, backdated or skipped by a modified client. For mutating admin operations, write the audit doc **in the same `writeBatch`** as the change. Enforce `timestamp == request.time` (use `serverTimestamp()`), a `hasOnly` whitelist, and `performedBy == request.auth.uid` in the rules. Add pagination to the audit modal (it shows only the latest 50).
- **S-4 Error leakage `[CODE]`:** `handleFirestoreError` throws `new Error(JSON.stringify({ error, authInfo: { userId, email, providerInfo... }, path }))` and many components render `err.message` directly. Users see raw JSON containing their UID/email and Firestore paths. Replace with `toUserMessage(err)` that maps Firebase codes (`permission-denied`, `unavailable`, `deadline-exceeded`, `resource-exhausted`, `failed-precondition`, `not-found`, `already-exists`, `auth/*`) to short friendly text, while the detailed object goes only to the structured logger (no email/UID in `console.error` in production).
- **S-5 Super-admin identity exposure `[CODE]`:** the super admin's personal Gmail is rendered to every member in `AdminLoginModal`. Remove it from user-facing text (rules may keep it; add a comment that moving to a custom claim is the long-term plan). Remove the personal-email fallback in `initializeFirestoreWithDefaults` and the `'rahatmdmahadihasan@gmail.com'` fallback in `handleSeedClubTemplate`.
- **S-6 Rate/abuse `[VERIFY]`:** any Google account can create a `users` doc and a few notifications. Implement Firebase **App Check** in code behind an env flag (`VITE_APPCHECK_SITE_KEY`; no behavior change when unset), and list the Console steps (register reCAPTCHA, monitor, then enforce for Firestore and Auth).
- **S-7 Web API key `[VERIFY]`:** in Google Cloud Console → APIs & Services → Credentials, restrict the browser key to the production origins and only the required APIs. Tell me the exact steps; do not paste the key.
- **S-8 Admin abuse controls `[CODE]`:** `checkIns` delete and `sessions`/`members` deletes are unrestricted for any admin. Require a non-empty reason in the UI for permanent deletes, write the audit entry in the same batch, and prefer soft delete. Super admin only for `grantAdminRole` already holds; also add a rule so admins cannot revoke the super admin and the last admin cannot be removed.
- **S-9 Dependency hygiene `[RUN]`:** do not apply `npm audit fix --force`. Run `npm ls @grpc/grpc-js` and report whether it ships in the browser bundle (inspect the `dist/` output). Pin and update to the newest `firebase` 12.x that resolves the advisory if one exists; otherwise document it as accepted risk.
- **S-10 Headers/transport `[CODE]`:** `server.ts` sets no security headers. Add (hand-written middleware, no new dependency): `Strict-Transport-Security`, `X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff`, `Referrer-Policy: strict-origin-when-cross-origin`, `Permissions-Policy: camera=(self), geolocation=(), microphone=()`, and a **Content-Security-Policy-Report-Only first**, then enforcing. It must allow: `self`; Firebase/Google APIs (`https://*.googleapis.com`, `https://*.firebaseapp.com`, `wss://*.firebaseio.com`, `https://apis.google.com`, `https://accounts.google.com`); Google Fonts (`fonts.googleapis.com` style, `fonts.gstatic.com` font); `img-src 'self' data: blob: https:`; `worker-src 'self'`; `media-src 'self' blob:` (camera). **Do not** set a restrictive `frame-ancestors` until you have confirmed how AI Studio embeds the preview; if it must remain embeddable, leave it out and say so.

## Data integrity
- **D-1 Orphans and cascades `[CODE]`:** `adminDeleteMember(permanent)` calls `permanentDeleteMember(memberId)` **without** `linkedAuthUid`, so the user doc stays `active` with a dangling `memberId` (the app then shows a workspace in which nothing works). Look up the linked UID (`members/{id}.authUid`) and reset the user (`status`, `memberId: deleteField()`, not `null`), in the same batch. Prefer deactivation (`Inactive`) as the default action; permanent deletion must keep historical check-ins (or anonymise them) and say what it does.
- **D-2 Non-atomic multi-step writes `[CODE]`:** `handleAddMember` (create member, then approve link), `acceptMembershipInvitation` follow-up audit, session create + announcement + audit, and `updateMemberProfile` + audit are separate awaits; a failure in the middle leaves partial state. Put each logical operation in one `writeBatch` (audit doc included). If a batch would exceed 500 writes, chunk and make each chunk idempotent.
- **D-3 Handle uniqueness `[CODE]`:** `addMemberToFirestore` checks `where('handle','==',h)` and then writes: a race, and case-sensitive (`@Rahat` vs `@rahat`). Add a `handles/{lowercaseHandle}` claim doc created in the same batch (rules: admin create, unique by doc ID; delete when the member is deleted/renamed). Normalise the leading `@`.
- **D-4 Statistics correctness `[CODE]`** (`stats.ts`, `storage.ts`):
  - `total` and `points` count every check-in even for **cancelled/deleted** sessions (deleted sessions fall back to 10 points). Count only check-ins whose session exists and is not cancelled.
  - `total` ignores the `joinedDate` eligibility used for streaks. Make all three metrics use the same eligibility rule.
  - `calculateMonthlyStats` builds `trackBreakdown` from `c.track`, a field that check-in writes never set, so every check-in is counted as "Hardware & Robotics". Derive the track from the session. It also uses `m.currentStreak`, a member field nothing ever updates; compute streaks via `computeMemberStats`.
  - `toJsDate(undefined | invalid)` returns **`new Date()`** (now), silently turning missing/corrupt timestamps into "this instant" and corrupting session state and statistics. Return `null`/an invalid sentinel and make callers handle it.
  - Timezone: `monthKey` is taken from `session.date`; ensure it is always the club-timezone calendar month and use `getMonthKeyInClubTz` consistently.
- **D-5 Concurrency `[CODE]`:** the token write on rotation is last-write-wins by one admin tab. If two admins open the kiosk for the same session, tokens fight each other. Detect an existing fresh token (`rotatedAt` < 60 s) on kiosk start and show "Another kiosk is active for this session. Take over?".
- **D-6 Backup/rollback `[VERIFY]`:** no backup exists in the repo. Add an admin-only "Export full backup (JSON)" (members, sessions, check-ins, announcements, settings; `memberPrivate` only if the admin chooses) and a CSV export that also neutralises tab/carriage-return leading characters (`sanitizeCSVCell` covers `= + - @` only). List the Console steps to enable Firestore point-in-time recovery and scheduled backups (billing required) and a restore drill.
- **D-7 Migrations:** follow DATA MIGRATION RULES.

